{"hash":"049d115b0efd0c6c","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":103.129,"said":"You have reached Shri Grishneshwar, the Lord of Ghushma. Look: a small, dark linga, worn smooth by countless hands, rising from its stone base in this low, lamp-lit room. This is the Lord who gave a mother back her son, and who spared the one who took him, because she asked. Fold your hands. Adi Shankaracharya sings of this place in the Dvadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram: ‘Ilapure ramyavishalake’smin samullasantam cha jagadvarenyam, vande mahodaratarasvabhavam Ghrishneshvarakhyam sharanam prapadye.’ ‘Here in Ilapura, lovely and wide, he shines forth, most worthy of the whole world’s worship. I bow to him, whose nature is boundlessly generous; to him called Ghrishneshwara I go for refuge.’ Grishneshwar is the twelfth and last Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. By tradition, a yatra of the twelve Jyotirlingas is held complete with this darshan. Pilgrims come here by many roads, in whatever order their lives allow. But remember now where the Stotram begins: at Somnath, the first, where the sea breaks on the shore. From that shore, through forest hills and river islands, ancient cities and Himalayan snow, the journey has brought you to this small room in a village beside Ellora. Whatever you have carried here, set it down. The circle is complete. Let the bells ring and the lamps be raised. Har Har Mahadev. Om Namah Shivaya.\n\nDid you know? The Shiva Purana says Shiva also blessed Ghushma’s family line to flourish for a hundred and one generations.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1328},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1344,"end":1453}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,3],[0.314,0.264,4,8],[0.591,0.385,9,16],[0.988,0.288,17,21],[1.288,0.865,22,34],[2.346,0.096,36,39],[2.454,0.325,40,44],[2.79,0.156,45,47],[2.959,0.517,48,55],[4.411,0.349,57,61],[4.964,0.06,63,64],[5.036,0.529,65,70],[5.697,0.373,72,76],[6.082,0.445,77,82],[6.683,0.288,84,88],[6.983,0.397,89,95],[7.392,0.18,96,98],[7.584,